# ATS Update — 2026-09-04 (overnight session, cont.) Found a real "stuck-in-place" collapse mode; added entropy instrumentation

## Context
Continuation of `updates/2026-09-04c ...`. Ran `py -3.12 main.py --no-gui
--episodes 2000 --fresh` under `REWARD_SCHEME_VERSION=2` (GAE added).

## Result: mostly healthy, but a real recurring failure mode found
Per-100-episode reward averages stayed positive and reasonably stable for
the first ~700 episodes (60-80 avg, all MID grade — much more consistent
than the pre-GAE 300-episode run). From ~episode 700 onward, a handful of
episodes spike to catastrophic negative reward (-225 to -931), dragging two
100-episode blocks (1301-1400, 1601-1700) to a negative average despite
being otherwise healthy. Grade distribution over all 2000: MID 1854, FAIR
128, GOOD 11, BAD 7 — the extreme negatives are rare but severe.

## Diagnosis
Looked at the worst cluster directly (episodes 1682-1689, 7 consecutive
episodes at reward ≈ -890 to -931, **all terminating at the exact same
tick count: 4470**). That repetition is the tell — not organic variance.

Checked the PPO update lines feeding those episodes: `actor` loss is
`0.0000` to `-0.0030` (rounds to zero) across ~17 consecutive updates
through the whole cluster, while `critic` loss also collapses toward ~0.01.
Reading `rewards.py`'s `update_standing_still`: the per-tick penalty for
not moving is capped at `R_STANDING_STILL * 4 = -0.2/tick` (deliberately,
so a stuck agent doesn't get a bottomless-pit gradient). `4470 * 0.2 ≈ 894`
— matches the observed magnitude closely.

Read together: the policy became near-deterministic (peaked enough that
entropy's pull is negligible) and landed on a "stand still" action from a
given start state. Because the environment reset is deterministic and the
policy is too, the exact same trajectory replays every episode. Once
plateaued at the -0.2/tick cap, consecutive-tick rewards are nearly
identical, so GAE temporal-difference deltas are ≈0 through the stuck
region — there is almost no gradient telling the policy to do anything
else until the eventual death signal, and even that is one sparse spike
diluted across a 256-step rollout. This is a real PPO collapse mode
(entropy-starved local trap), not a reward-shaping bug — the cap on
`R_STANDING_STILL` is doing its job (bounding the penalty), it's just
that a bounded-but-flat penalty region also means a flat gradient region.

It self-recovers within a handful of episodes each time (by ep 1701-1800
the run is back to 46 avg, all MID) — an on-policy exploration hiccup, not
a permanent collapse. But it recurred in at least 3 separate clusters
across the 2000 episodes (ep ~960, ~1355-1385, ~1682-1690, ~1979), so it's
worth being able to see directly rather than infer from tick counts.

## Change (instrumentation only — no hyperparameter tuning yet)
- `mind/continual_learner.py`: `_do_ppo_update` now also tracks
  `entropy.mean()` and prints `adv_mean` / `adv_std` for the batch, so a
  future stuck cluster can be confirmed directly (is entropy actually
  collapsing, is advantage actually flatlining) instead of inferred from
  tick counts and loss magnitudes after the fact.
- `debug_gui.py`: `record_loss()` widened to accept the new 4th
  (`entropy`) value from `learner.last_losses` — **without this, GUI mode
  would have raised `TypeError: record_loss() takes 4 positional arguments
  but 5 were given` on the very first PPO update**, since `main.py` calls
  `gui.record_loss(*learner.last_losses)`. Caught by a syntax/AST check
  across the changed files before relaunching, not by running the GUI
  itself (headless overnight session — GUI untested tonight).

## Deliberately NOT done tonight
Did not touch `ENTROPY_COEFF` or the standing-still penalty shape. The
project's own history already shows a blind entropy-coefficient bump
(0.01→0.005) being reverted for a different reason ("too strong exploration
for small action spaces") — changing it again without the entropy telemetry
above to confirm the actual collapse mechanism would be exactly the
"tuned symptoms instead of causes" mistake this project already made once
(see `docs/architecture_and_reasoning_2026-09-04.md`). Next run's entropy
numbers should say whether entropy really is collapsing before touching
`ENTROPY_COEFF` or considering an annealing schedule.

